# C Style Guide for Roguelike Development

This guide outlines C coding standards optimized for roguelike development in BrogueMCP. It focuses on code readability, cross-platform compatibility, and performance considerations for grid-based games.

## Formatting and Layout

### Indentation and Spacing

- Use 4 spaces for indentation, not tabs
- Maximum line length is 120 characters
- Add spaces after commas in parameter lists
- No space between function name and opening parenthesis
- One space after keywords like `if`, `for`, `while`, etc.

```c
void functionName(int parameter1, int parameter2)
{
    if (condition) {
        // Code here
    }
}
```

### Braces

- Opening braces on the same line for control statements
- Opening braces on a new line for function definitions
- Always use braces for control statements, even for single-line blocks

```c
// Function definition
void functionName(int parameter)
{
    // Function body
}

// Control statement
if (condition) {
    doSomething();
}
```

## Naming Conventions

- `camelCase` for variable and function names
- `UPPER_CASE` for constants and macros
- `PascalCase` for type definitions, structs, and enums
- Prefix global variables with `g_`
- Use descriptive, meaningful names that convey purpose

```c
#define MAX_DUNGEON_DEPTH 100

typedef struct DungeonCell {
    int terrainType;
} DungeonCell;

int g_currentDepth = 0;

void createRoom(int width, int height);
```

## Common Roguelike Patterns

### Grid-Based Operations

- Use 2D arrays for maps/grids, with consistent coordinate systems
- Document coordinate systems clearly (origin, axes directions)
- Use `typedef` for grid cell types

```c
typedef struct Cell {
    int terrain;
    int entity;
    int item;
    // Other properties
} Cell;

Cell dungeonMap[MAX_HEIGHT][MAX_WIDTH];

// Access pattern
Cell* getCell(int x, int y) {
    if (x >= 0 && x < MAX_WIDTH && y >= 0 && y < MAX_HEIGHT) {
        return &dungeonMap[y][x];
    }
    return NULL;
}
```

### Memory Management

- Prefer stack allocation for temporary data
- Use consistent allocation/deallocation patterns
- Check all memory allocations for success

```c
// Preferred pattern
SomeStruct* createObject(int size) {
    SomeStruct* obj = malloc(sizeof(SomeStruct));
    if (obj == NULL) {
        return NULL;  // Handle error
    }
    
    // Initialize
    obj->data = malloc(size * sizeof(int));
    if (obj->data == NULL) {
        free(obj);  // Clean up previous allocation
        return NULL;
    }
    
    return obj;
}

void destroyObject(SomeStruct* obj) {
    if (obj != NULL) {
        free(obj->data);
        free(obj);
    }
}
```

### Random Number Generation

- Use a consistent RNG system throughout the code
- Document the RNG behavior and seed management
- Consider platform differences in RNG implementation

```c
// Preferred pattern
int randomInt(int min, int max) {
    // Ensure good distribution
    return min + (int)(randomFloat() * (max - min + 1));
}

float randomFloat() {
    // Implementation with good statistical properties
    return (float)rand() / RAND_MAX;
}
```

## Comments and Documentation

- Use clear, concise comments to explain "why", not just "what"
- Document parameters, return values, and side effects for functions
- Use consistent header documentation format

```c
/**
 * Creates a new room in the dungeon.
 * 
 * @param x The x-coordinate of the top-left corner
 * @param y The y-coordinate of the top-left corner
 * @param width The width of the room
 * @param height The height of the room
 * @return 1 if room was created successfully, 0 otherwise
 */
int createRoom(int x, int y, int width, int height);
```

## Cross-Platform Considerations

- Avoid platform-specific code; use abstractions when necessary
- Be careful with assumptions about integer sizes
- Use standard library functions when possible
- Test on all target platforms

```c
// Preferred
#include <stdint.h>

void function(int32_t value) {
    // This will have consistent behavior across platforms
}

// Avoid
void function(long value) {
    // Size of long varies between 32-bit and 64-bit platforms
}
```

## Error Handling

- Check return values from functions that can fail
- Use consistent error reporting patterns
- Propagate errors up the call stack when appropriate

```c
int loadDungeon(const char* filename) {
    FILE* file = fopen(filename, "r");
    if (file == NULL) {
        logError("Failed to open file: %s", filename);
        return 0;  // Error code
    }
    
    // Load data
    
    fclose(file);
    return 1;  // Success
}
```

## Optimization

- Focus on readability first, optimize only when necessary
- Profile before optimizing
- Document optimization techniques and reasoning
- Be cautious with pointer arithmetic and unsafe optimizations

Follow these guidelines to maintain a consistent, readable, and robust codebase for BrogueMCP development.